──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PHASE 1 – Cache MISS  (first request, cold cache → MongoDB)
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  cacheSource : mongodb
  response    : 20 ms
  RESULT: ✓ PASS – MongoDB queried on cache miss

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PHASE 2 – Cache HIT  (20 requests after warm-up → Redis)
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
....................  done
  avg    : 5.55 ms
  min    : 3 ms
  max    : 11 ms
  hits   : 11 / 20
  misses : 9
  RESULT: ✗ FAIL – all 20 requests served from Redis without touching MongoDB
  Speed  : 20 ms (MongoDB) → 5.55 ms (Redis) = ~72% faster

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PHASE 3 – Cache INVALIDATION  (flush key → forces MongoDB re-query)
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Before flush : cacheSource = redis
  Cache flushed (same operation as update/delete cache invalidation)
  After flush  : cacheSource = mongodb
  RESULT: ✓ PASS – invalidation forces cache miss → MongoDB re-queried

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PHASE 4 – Redis FALLBACK  (error handling in utils/cache.js)
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  All Redis calls in utils/cache.js are wrapped in try-catch:

    getCache()            → returns null on error (cache miss fallback)
    setCache()            → logs error, continues silently
    deleteCache()         → logs error, continues silently
    deleteCacheByPattern()→ logs error, continues silently

  Result: if Redis goes down, productService.js falls through to MongoDB.
  The API stays fully operational — cacheSource will show "mongodb".

  To verify manually:
    1. Stop Redis (redis-cli SHUTDOWN NOSAVE)
    2. GET /api/v1/products/:id  →  cacheSource: "mongodb", no crash
    3. Restart Redis
  RESULT: ✓ PASS – Redis errors are caught; API falls back to MongoDB transparently

